Just to give examples:

I would allow rereading spellbooks at any time, resetting the expiry 
time of the spell to the value you get by first reading the book.  The 
way it works now (if I'm up to date :-) ) that "you know the spell quite 
well already" without giving you the chance to relearn it, if you are 
not close enough to expiry time, creates the awkward situation that you 
either have to carry spellbooks to the planes when you are not sure that 
you are approaching the expiry time or you have to wait for expiry of 
all the spells you want to use with certainty in the endgame and to 
relearn them briefly before ascension.  This means that you may not have 
an important spell at your disposal for quite some time.  Also, carrying 
the spellbooks along may not be practical anyway, as you will not have 
the time to study the spellbooks on the planes...

Therefore, I would allow relearning of spells at any time without 
additional costs (beyond the loss of the additional time that you might 
  have used the spell before relearning it which is an inherent cost). 
An additional rationale is of course also that in reality you can 
relearn anything that you learn from a book without having to forget it 
first or to get very close to forgetting...

As to an improvable situation that should be made more difficult without 
any "benefit" for compensation, I would suggest to make Vlad more 
difficult.  He is such a wimp right now that this calls for improvement. 
  One way would be to make his level drain attacks succeed more 
frequently.  Let magic resistance protect you only 10% of the time 
instead of 95%.  If you give him enough hitpoints in addition to make 
him survive long enough to attack you 10 times that would mean that even 
a well-armed player would generally lose 9 levels to him, suggesting 
that Vlad should not be taken on in melee anymore...
